is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the reported outage. End-to-end reachability between the hosts is currently healthy: pc_0_0 can ping pc_0_1 with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT, and vice versa. The control plane across the fabric is also up. On spine_router_0_0, BGP is fully established to all neighbors (super spine 172.16.0.0 and leaves 172.16.0.5/172.16.0.7), with prefixes exchanged and stable timers (Up/Down ~00:01:40). Both leaves have the necessary remote subnet via BGP installed: leaf_router_0_0 has 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4 (eth0), and leaf_router_0_1 has 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6 (eth0). The spines’ routing tables carry both leaf subnets as expected, and the super-spine is peered and advertising both networks to the spines.

Mechanism: With all BGP adjacencies established and correct routes present on each hop, traffic from 10.0.0.2 to 10.0.1.2 forwards normally through the fabric. If spine_router_0_0 had “crashed,” the affected leaf would have lost its BGP path; that is not the case.

Localization: No device shows missing routes or down links. spine_router_0_0 is operational and forwarding.

Root cause: No active fault found; the “spine crash” assertion is not supported by current data.

Corrective fix: Do not reboot any spine. Close the ticket as not reproducible. If the issue occurred overnight and cleared, consider reviewing logs/telemetry for transient events and enabling alerting, but no immediate remediation is required.
